&lt;div class=&quot;hs-featured-image-wrapper&quot;&gt; 
 &lt;a class=&quot;hs-featured-image-link&quot; href=&quot;https://blog.pcisecuritystandards.org/pci-ssc-publishes-new-guidance-on-understanding-pci-dss-third-party-service-providers&quot; title=&quot;&quot;&gt; &lt;img alt=&quot;PCI SSC Publishes New Guidance on Understanding PCI DSS Third-Party Service Providers &quot; class=&quot;hs-featured-image&quot; src=&quot;https://blog.pcisecuritystandards.org/hubfs/2026-blog/tpsp-blog.jpg&quot; style=&quot;width: auto !important; float: left; margin: 0 15px 15px 0;&quot; /&gt; &lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;/div&gt; 
&lt;br /&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The PCI Security Standards Council (PCI SSC) has released a new information supplement, &lt;span style=&quot;color: #0a5d66;&quot;&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://docs-prv.pcisecuritystandards.org/PCI%20DSS/Supporting%20Document/Understanding-PCI-DSS-Service-Providers-v1_0.pdf&quot; style=&quot;font-weight: bold; color: #0a5d66;&quot;&gt;&lt;em&gt;Understanding PCI DSS Service Providers&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;, to help merchants, service providers, assessors, and acquirers understand PCI DSS requirements and expectations for relationships between customers and third-party service providers (TPSPs). The Council developed this guidance in partnership with the PCI SSC Board of Advisors and Global Executive Assessor Roundtable (GEAR) and in response to questions received about TPSP relationships. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It&amp;#8217;s a &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/openssh-flaw-lets-attackers-log-into-password-disabled-linux-accounts/&quot;&gt;bad one&lt;/a&gt;, and it&amp;#8217;s been in the code for a decade:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;&lt;p&gt;A newly disclosed flaw involving OpenSSH and musl, dubbed &amp;#8220;*sshole,&amp;#8221; allows attackers to log in to certain Alpine Linux and other musl-based Linux accounts configured to block password authentication simply by entering an oversized password.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;#8220;Oversized&amp;#8221; means longer than 256 bytes. &lt;a href=&quot;https://sshhole.com/&quot;&gt;Details&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Slashdot &lt;a href=&quot;https://it.slashdot.org/story/26/11/05/2140208/openssh-flaw-lets-attackers-log-into-password-disabled-linux-accounts&quot;&gt;thread&lt;/a&gt;. And another news &lt;a href=&quot;https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/11/vulnerability-allows-attackers-to-log-into-some-linux-devices-sans-password/&quot;&gt;article&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;News story: &amp;#8220;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2026/10/261019093434.htm&quot;&gt;Scientists discover a bizarre Jurassic &amp;#8216;squid&amp;#8217; unlike anything ever seen&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;#8221;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;&lt;p&gt;Scientists have identified the &amp;#8220;fattest&amp;#8221; belemnite ever discovered, a bizarre, barrel-shaped squid relative that lived in Wyoming&amp;#8217;s ancient seas 160 million years ago. The exceptionally rare fossils reveal a creature unlike anything previously known, potentially a predator of smaller squid-like animals and a surprising survivor of a supposedly vanished family.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;As usual, you can also use this squid post to talk about the security stories in the news that I haven&amp;#8217;t covered...&lt;/p&gt;
